   "text": {
    "L1": "A pictograph uses pictures to show data. First, read the key.",
    "L2": "The key tells us how many items one picture stands for, so we must not count each picture as one item.",
    "L3": "To find the total, multiply the number of pictures by the key value.",
    "L4": "A half picture stands for half the key value. So half of 10 is 5.",
    "L5": "For 45 kites with a key of 10 kites per picture, divide 45 by 10: that gives 4 whole pictures and 5 left over.",
    "L6": "The 5 left over is half of 10, so the pictograph row has 4 and a half pictures.",
    "L7": "Keep every picture the same size and evenly spaced, so rows can be compared fairly.",
    "L8": "Now use the key, count the pictures including any half picture, and calculate the total."
   },
